I have a few assumptions made in each test under the [PexAssumeUnderTest] attribute. But is there a way to programmatically make assumptions about my method inputs? I have found the [PexAssumeUnderTest] attribute very helpful in setting up some of my method contracts, but now I wish to have more control over them.
I would like to know if there's a way to add assumptions at method signature level so that my assumptions are part of the generated test cases directly.
The API I'm testing is designed in such a way that every method has different behavior based on the method parameters and it would be very helpful to have the behavior of each test case defined clearly at the method call site or test method signature.

A: No, you can not directly add assumptions at the method signature level in Pex. The Pex tool is designed to analyze the public and protected parts of the method, and not private or internal parts.

Assumptions are generally used to provide explicit conditions for the tool’s behavior. They allow you to specify what inputs you expect before you start your tests.

So, if you want to add assumptions, you can use the [PexAssumeParameter] attribute which is useful for controlling the tests based on the input parameters. Also, [PexMethod] attribute allows you to specify the parameters that should be tested. 

Remember, the assumption should be based on the behavior not based on preconditions which you can specify using [PexAssumePrecondition] attribute.

A: PEX does not support assumptions at method-level for complex reasons. The PEX framework has to analyze the method in isolation, which is practically impossible for something like adding assumptions based on input method parameters. It's too complex and expensive for PEX to try and add such fine-grained control. It might be possible, on a case-by-case basis, with [PexAssumeNoNullValue], [PexAssumeNotNull], [PexAssumeNotNull], [PexAssumeNotSideEffect], [PexAssumeNCIEF] attributes but PEX should not have control over other parts of system for its purpose.
If you want assumptions about method inputs, you are suggested to specify them through [PexAssumeParameter] attribute.

A: As you've correctly pointed out, the limitations of Pex include the inability to specify assumptions on the method's input parameters beyond just the preconditions, null, not-null values. 

While testing, you'll have to take care of those assumptions via the [PexAssumeParameter], [PexAssumeNotNull], [PexAssumeNotSideEffect] and similar attributes, which in turn require you to manually set up the scenario that your test is going to validate.

What you might want to consider is generating your own unit tests which validate a wide range of scenarios and cover the assumptions you want to set. I imagine that for complex object-like structures, this would also be cumbersome, but at least you could do more fine-grained checks. 

Unfortunately, until Microsoft provides a more comprehensive solution to this problem, there isn't a clear way to programmatically specify assumptions in Pex tests. The tool is inherently designed to test method behaviors without prior knowledge about the method's inputs or outputs.

Here's a workaround you can consider

1. Create a separate unit test project.
2. In your original project, create a public wrapper method that contains the assumptions.
3. In your new project, create your unit tests for the original methods and pass in your preconditions.

This way, you'll reap the benefits of Pex for your original project while still having full control over your unit tests. Just make sure that you consider all the different paths in your tests, and avoid complex setups when possible.

In short, Microsoft provides a more comprehensive solution for this as part of next version of Pex but for now, there is no direct way to do it.

