A: We can use below assertions in our methods to ensure that certain properties in the ArrayList object do not change upon calling certain methods. For example:

- [Test]
  public void Test_IndexOf()
  {
     ArrayList list = new ArrayList();
     list.Add(5);
     list.Add(4);
     // Check that list.IndexOf(4) does not give the same result after the Add operation
     Assert.AreEqual(1, list.IndexOf(4)); 
  }

In above example, we have checked that IndexOf(4) returns 1. After calling list.Add(4), IndexOf(4) should still return 1. 

Note that above assertions are testing code logic and not the contracts of the ArrayList class under PEX. Thus, these assertions ensure that your code logic is correct but does not ensure that the behaviors of ArrayList under PEX (like the properties Count, IndexOf, Contains, etc.) under PEX are also ensured. 

If you want to ensure the contracts of ArrayList, then you would need to do much more comprehensive tests using PexClass and PexMethod attributes and verify the fields/properties that Pex is analyzing in the test cases.

Also, as a guideline, testing a specific class should focus on testing that class only; i.e., you might have different test classes focusing on testing of other classes (not part of a class being tested).

Also, one thing to note is that the Pex generated test may not cover all potential paths in the ArrayList class. That's why additional manual tests for edge cases might be required.

We can only conduct unit testing for a specific function or a specific use case. For example, when we add an element to the List using Add(element) function, we expect the Count of the list to increase by 1. And so, we write a Unit test for this condition.

In summary, it is recommended to follow testing guidelines to ensure that each unit of the codebase works as expected when performing specific tasks.

For your question, if you want your test to ensure that the ArrayList does not change its Count property when calling Remove method, you can use the below test:

   [Test]
   public void Test_Remove()
   {
      ArrayList list = new ArrayList(){5, 4, 3, 2, 1};
      int oldCount = list.Count;
      list.Remove(5);
      Assert.AreEqual(oldCount, list.Count);  
   }

In above example, we have checked that the Count of the list remains the same after removing an element.

We can continue this approach, to check other properties as well.

Hope this answer is helpful.

A: In unit tests, we always assume that the system under test behaves as expected. For instance, when you call Remove(x), we assume that ArrayList should remove the first occurrence of x from the list if it exists.
As for contracts verification, Pex is not designed to check those. It focuses primarily on fuzz testing, code coverage, etc., and in practice, it may find bugs that are difficult to reproduce with unit tests. 
Unit tests, on the other hand, are typically very specific to a function or operation, i.e., they test one small part of your code. They will either fail if the code under test behaves unexpectedly or pass if it behaves as expected.
Therefore, while it seems that in some cases Pex and unit tests could be used together to cover a wide range of possible behaviors, there typically isn't a requirement to have an equivalent unit test for each Pex generated test case. 
Instead, Pex is often used for stress testing and finding edge cases that your unit tests might overlook. 
But please note, you should still have sufficient unit tests to handle all major functionality and edge cases. Tools like Pex help to find edge cases, but not all of them.
Hope that helps.

A: You have to balance between unit tests and contract testing. Tests--------------------	    PexObserve.VerifyNonAssertion(()=>{ System.Diagnostics.Debug.Assert(Old_arrListCount == New_arrListCount);  }); 
        }

        [PexMethod]
        public void PUT_RemoveAtContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int index)
        {
            AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(index < arrList.Count && index >= 0);

            int Old_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int Old_index = index;

            arrList.RemoveAt(index);

            int New_arrListCount = arrList.Count;

            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_Count", Old_arrListCount);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_Count", New_arrListCount);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_index", Old_index);

            // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that indicates at least one element of the array list is present in the original list
            PexObserve.VerifyNonAssertion(()=>{ System.Diagnostics.Debug.Assert(Old_arrListCount > New_arrListCount);  }); 

          // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that indicates the removed element exists and is not present at the index in the new list.
            PexObserve.VerifyNonAssertion(()=> {
              if (New_arrListCount <= Old_index)
                System.Diagnostics.Debug.Assert(true);
              else 
                System.Diagnostics.Debug.Assert((int)arrList[Old_index] != Old_index); 
            });  
        }

        [PexMethod]
        public void PUT_ClearContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList)
        {

            int Old_arrListCount = arrList.Count;

            arrList.Clear();

            int New_arrListCount = arrList.Count;

            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_Count", Old_arrListCount);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_Count", New_arrListCount);

            // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that indicates the array list is cleared
            PexObserve.VerifyNonAssertion(()=>{ System.Diagnostics.Debug.Assert(New_arrListCount == 0);  }); 
                      
        }
    }
}

